    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d263">

                <pb facs="0544" n="518" xml:id="pg518"/>

                <head>263. Memorandum for the Record of Discussion at the Daily White House Staff
                        Meeting<anchor n="1" xml:id="d263-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1963-11-01T08:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 1,
                                1963, 8 a.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Mr. Bundy presided throughout the meeting.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item><hi rendition="#italic">Vietnam.</hi> Bundy opened the meeting by stating
                        that “they” (presumably himself and Forrestal) had spent a quiet night
                        watching the cables from Vietnam. I asked how many forces were actually
                        involved, hoping to draw out some of Bundy’s thinking. He responded by
                        saying that, with the exception of the palace guard, the army in Saigon and
                        in the field seem to be firmly behind the Generals. Forrestal added that it
                        was a well executed coup, much better than anyone would have thought
                        possible. Bundy then commented that Diem was still holding out in the
                        palace, adding that no one wanted to go in for the kill. They preferred that
                        Diem leave the country. He continued that the Lodge trip is obviously off,
                        and concluded by saying that all we could do for the present was continue to
                        watch. At one point Dungan jokingly said that if we recognized this military
                        regime quickly, it would put him in trouble in Latin America. Someone
                        responded that the group in Vietnam was “representative.” Bundy, I believe,
                        added that this was an example of the acceptable type of military coup
                        Martin of State had in mind when he made his statement some weeks
                            ago.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d263-f2"/></item>
                </list>
                <p>[Here follows discussion of matters unrelated to Vietnam.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
